# Job dependencies

> Ron, a lot of times I need to submitted jobs to depend on one another.
> For
> instance, I can’t run a cycle depletion job until after the fuel shuffle
> jobs finishes successfully.  I usually use a qsub option to do this:
>
>
>
> qsub -W depend=afterok:152062.mod-condo-pbs01 run
>
>
>
> In this case, the new job tied to the run script is held until job 152062
> finishes.

> You could even stack serial jobs like:
>
>
>
> ID=qsub run
>
> ID=qsub -W depend=afterok:$ID run
>
> ID=qsub -W depend=afterok:$ID run
>
> ID=qsub -W depend=afterok:$ID run
>
> Etc…

Adding dependency on a job for which you know the ID is easy.
A stack of jobs with different .inp or .xml files seems easy enough.
Give me an example or two of how you want the command line to look.

Thinking while typing..   You could pass multiple .inp or .xml files on the
command line, optionally specifying a subdir for each with -s and/or a
a dependency with -d/--depend
Perhaps adding a '--serial' flag would specify a job stack.

## single job a dependency, the whatever.inp job depends on job 6275

    $ verarun -d 6275 whatever.inp

## parallel job submissions, the second in a subdir with a dependency on a
  previously submitted job

    $ verarun one.inp -d 6275 -s job2 two.inp three.inp


## serial job submissions, mean the second depends on the first, the third
  on the second, and so on

    $ verarun --serial one.inp -s job2 two.inp three.inp -s job4 four.inp

In this case

- four.inp depends on three.inp and is run in subdir job4/

- three.inp depends on two.inp

- two.inp depends on one.inp and runs in subdir job2/
